I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, and the frozen reference/index tooling. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build a full multiphase GSAS-II model, checkpoint the physical solution, and refine only supported parameters before exporting the requested files.

The chemistry narrows the first-pass inventory to Zn–W–O products plus residual ZnO/WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now extracting the relevant frozen references and comparing their calculated peak positions/intensities against the observed coherent peak families.

The dominant peak family is consistent with a wolframite-type zinc tungstate rather than scheelite: its characteristic low-angle and split 24–31° groups are all present. The exact ZnWO₄ composition is absent from the frozen index, so I’m covering both major ABX₄ topologies using chemically nearest parents, then will transfer only the physically valid one to Zn and compare in the same fit.

The transferred wolframite model reproduces every major observed family with a common ≈+0.3° specimen/calibration shift; scheelite misses the diagnostic split groups. Before refinement, I’m checking same-cation molybdate/related analogues for better Zn-site and oxygen-coordinate starts, which should reduce bias from using MnWO₄ coordinates.

The controlled topology comparison is decisive: wolframite reaches 30.7% Rwp before profile relaxation, while scheelite stalls near 57.1% and leaves most peak families unindexed. I have preserved the physical wolframite checkpoint. One attempted simultaneous zero/height refinement exposed their expected near-perfect correlation, so I’m rolling that back and will refine a single position-correction term, with bounded physical profile parameters.

The bounded profile and staged coordinate refinement improved Rwp from 30.7% to 19.5%. Heavy-atom shifts are modest; the oxygen step is larger and therefore provisional. I’m geometry-screening that checkpoint now and will roll it back if ZnO₆/WO₆ distances or polyhedra are implausible before testing specimen effects.

The unconstrained oxygen improvement fails the physical screen (W–O extends to 2.32 Å), and the attempted hard-bound mechanism leaves stale calculated arrays, so that branch is rejected. I’m returning to the heavy-atom checkpoint and testing only the five allowed March–Dollase axes, plus polarization/surface terms separately, before reconsidering any small oxygen adjustment.

The [111] preferred-orientation term is strongly supported (Rwp 22.5% → 18.2%, March–Dollase 0.717). Polarization improves this to 17.54% at the physical upper bound; adding surface roughness does not improve the model and is rejected. I’m now optimizing the single phase-wide displacement factor, then I’ll revisit oxygen positions under the corrected intensity model.

With the supported orientation/polarization corrections in place, refining both oxygen sites now gives a physical octahedral model (W–O 1.84–2.18 Å; Zn–O 1.95–2.24 Å) and Rwp 17.25%. I’m treating this as the current physical checkpoint and mapping its residual maxima to the calculated reflection list to decide whether any second phase is warranted.

The residual map reveals two coherent precursor families, not unexplained single peaks: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for 23.41/23.98/24.67/33.60/34.49/41.57°, while ZnO accounts for 32.06/34.91/47.92/56.79/68.25°. This also closes the 1:1 precursor molar balance. I’m now comparing distinct WO₃ symmetry/topology parents in the same ZnWO₄ + ZnO + WO₃ state.

The first topology batch exposed a GSAS-II scripting hazard: clearing frozen variables autosaved the shared source project, so parallel branches collided. No accepted checkpoint was lost; I’m restoring from its backup, giving each branch its own project before any mutation, and rerunning serially/isolated.

The isolated monoclinic branch is stable at Rwp 9.92% with positive scales for all three phases. The triclinic branch is computationally slower because it has many more independent W/oxygen sites; it is still refining, and I’m retaining it until the comparable checkpoint completes.

The low-symmetry comparison is now clear: monoclinic gives 9.92% Rwp; triclinic gives 9.53% only by shearing its published 88.81° angle to 90.07°, effectively collapsing toward the monoclinic metric. Orthorhombic remains at 12.21% with size/strain pinned at bounds. I’m finishing the high-symmetry controls, then will refine the physically consistent monoclinic branch.

The residual families are now fully assigned to wolframite ZnWO₄ plus trace ZnO and monoclinic WO₃. I’m refining the light-atom coordinates cautiously from that physical checkpoint, then I’ll freeze the best replay and generate all requested outputs directly from it.

The quantitative GSAS-II mass fractions at the current checkpoint are about 82.1% ZnWO₄, 5.4% ZnO, and 12.5% WO₃. Before accepting them, I’m removing one collapsed broadening parameter and testing whether the weak phases actually support any oxygen-coordinate refinement; unsupported light-atom freedom will be rolled back.

The first bounded-coordinate trial exposed an API detail: GSAS-II treats its `dAx/dAy/dAz` controls as absolute fractional coordinates, not coordinate shifts. I stopped that branch; the accepted stabilized checkpoint was unaffected. I’m rerunning with bounds centered on each literature coordinate.

Both weak-phase oxygen tests failed the reliability screen: ZnO’s O coordinate ran to its bound with crystallite size also collapsing, and all WO₃ oxygen coordinates tried to sit on bounds. I’m retaining their reference oxygen geometry. The stable three-phase model converges cleanly at Rwp 9.923%; I’m now checking assigned-peak residuals for one defensible texture or anisotropic-width correction.

The weak-phase texture screen is giving only marginal changes so far (ΔRwp ≤0.06 percentage point), unlike the strongly supported ZnWO₄ [111] texture term. I’m completing the finite axis check to avoid accepting a noise-driven minor-phase correction.

The only physically bounded line-broadening improvement is ZnWO₄ uniaxial microstrain along [001] (about 523 ppm equatorial and 1410 ppm axial); the nominally lower-Rwp [010] branch demanded an implausible 0.07 axial/equatorial ratio at its bound and is being rejected. I’m converging the [001] branch and geometry-screening the resulting structures.

Geometry screening passes for ZnWO₄ and ZnO. The selected old monoclinic WO₃ reference, however, has one W site with only five short W–O contacts and two near 2.9 Å; that limitation was already present in the source coordinates. I’m checking only a few exact-composition monoclinic WO₃ records for a chemically physical parent, as required before final acceptance.

The modern, geometry-valid WO₃ parent fits better even before optional terms (Rwp 9.626% versus 9.923% for the old coordinates). Its [100] texture axis is emerging as a supported correction; I’m finishing the axis controls, then will converge that branch before comparing profile terms.

The geometry-valid branch is now converged at Rwp 9.252% with no frozen variables. It gives 82.47% ZnWO₄, 4.92% ZnO, and 12.61% WO₃, and the residual precursor amounts nearly close the original 1:1 Zn:W balance. I’m doing one final bounded WO₃ oxygen test; it will only be retained if all coordinates remain interior and octahedral geometry stays sound.

The final ZnO texture retest is now stable: [001] gives a modest but reproducible improvement without crystallite-size collapse (MD 0.865, size 0.050 µm). The competing basal axes fit essentially the same but require much larger, less stable corrections, so I’m retaining the physically common ZnO c-axis term and converging the complete model.

The complete model is stable and all optional branches are resolved. I’m now producing `final.gpx`, the three phase CIFs, the full-grid fit profile, and the normalized phase table directly from one last replay of that accepted checkpoint.

A replay audit caught one contamination from the rejected oxygen trial: GSAS-II autosaved the first WO₃ oxygen flag into an intermediate checkpoint, so the later ZnO-texture comparison was not isolated. I’ve traced the clean lineage to the pre-test checkpoint and am rebuilding the last two stages from it; the current submitted files will be replaced only after this clean replay passes.

The clean replay passed: 8,301 profile rows exactly match the original grid and observations, the independently recomputed Rwp is 9.198%, fractions sum to 1.000000, all CIFs parse with valid geometry, and `final.gpx` reopens converged with no frozen variables.

The crystalline phase model is:

- ZnWO₄ wolframite: 82.645 wt%
- ZnO wurtzite: 4.883 wt%
- Monoclinic γ-WO₃: 12.472 wt%

Final fit: Rwp 9.198%, Rp 5.115%, GOF 3.485. The refinement converged without frozen variables. Detailed ZnO and WO₃ oxygen coordinates remain reference-constrained, as explained in the report.

